internal/monty vendors the Go wrapper for the Pydantic Monty Python
interpreter (https://github.com/fugue-labs/monty-go), taken at release
v0.2.0 (commit c5fede1ca39482ff3da7dc04c5fd569b8bc313b8, tag v0.2.0; the
vendored tree was checked out from the tag itself). Monty is distributed
under the MIT License; the LICENSE
file taken alongside the code is the upstream one and applies to this
directory rather than the repository-root licence.

The package code was copied and only mechanically changed: the package name
was rewritten from `montygo` to `monty` and error strings from
`montygo: …` to `monty: …`. Otherwise the files are upstream verbatim:

  * monty.go   — Runner, Limits, FunctionCall/FuncDef, Execute options
  * wasm.go    — the wazero instance plumbing and the pause/resume for
                 external function calls
  * errors.go  — MontyError

The upstream tests (monty_test.go) are not vendored; they use only the
standard library, but codetool_test.go in internal/coder covers the
behavior Strument depends on.

**The monty.wasm blob is NOT built from source here.** Rebuilding it needs
a Rust toolchain with the `wasm32-wasip1` target, and Strument deliberately
requires none (no cgo, no C toolchain — see CLAUDE.md). The rebuild
procedure and the shim changes it needs are written out in
[`REBUILD.md`](REBUILD.md), aimed at models and humans, keyed by compile
error so mild-to-moderate upstream divergence can be absorbed without
re-deriving the port. Last done September 2026, from monty-go commit
c5fede1 with the shim's `monty` git dependency bumped to the Pydantic Monty
v0.0.21 tag.

1. Clone https://github.com/fugue-labs/monty-go and check out the commit
   named above; apply the changes in [`REBUILD.md`](REBUILD.md).
2. `rustup target add wasm32-wasip1`
3. `make build` — the Makefile passes `--remap-path-prefix` for the build
   directory and the cargo home (Rust's `-trimpath`), and strips DWARF, so
   the blob embeds no local paths. If the build directory differs from
   `/tmp/monty-go`, the absolute prefix in the Makefile's `RUSTFLAGS` must
   match.
4. Copy `monty.wasm` into this directory and update the SHA-256 below.

The blob is reproducible on the same toolchain: three cold builds into
separate `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`s produced the identical hash. Nuance: the
reproducibility gate is the rustc version and the resolved dependency
versions — the shim builds against git dependencies and the monty tag moves,
so two machines agree only with the same `Cargo.lock` (vendored with the
shim, but cargo updates the git checkout's lockfile entries when the tag
resolves differently) and the same rustc. The path part — the part Go
`-trimpath` solves — is fully handled by the remap.

  * monty.wasm — SHA-256
    c05e79b5e163df7196ff54966da6291823a3748d788c4191be657918bac2ee9b

The shim changes for the v0.0.11 → v0.0.21 API (kept locally; not
upstreamed) are the ones described in [`REBUILD.md`](REBUILD.md): types
split out into the new `monty_types` crate, the generic `<LimitedTracker>`
parameter dropped from `RunProgress` and the snapshot structs,
`MontyRun::new` gaining a `CompileOptions` argument, and `OsCall` now
carrying a typed `OsFunctionCall` (unpacked with `to_args()`). The C-ABI
wire format to the Go wrapper is unchanged. One behavioral change:
`max_allocations` is no longer enforced (upstream moved allocation
accounting into the `monty-alloc` global allocator, which the shim does not
arm) — `max_duration` and `max_recursion_depth` still are. Classes are now
supported; the tool description's wall list moved `classes` to the
available column accordingly.

Upgrading Monty — new wrapper code or a new blob — is a separate decision
with its own cost, and a reviewer should not have to guess that. The
wrapper is ~3 files by a single upstream author, which is why it is
vendored rather than taken as a module dependency.

The only dependencies are github.com/tetratelabs/wazero (pure Go) and
golang.org/x/sys.
